{"id":"ed0d1dc1-d821-47b4-b0da-7f417bdea628","object":"chat.completion","created":1793336523,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"I have the complete mechanisms exposed; now I'll verify helper definitions in environ.py before editing.\n\n```python\nr = read_range(\"deepinfer/environ.py\", 1, 30)\nprint(r[\"content\"])\nprint(\"HASH\", r[\"file_hash\"])\n```","reasoning_content":"Let me proceed with implementing. I have the mechanisms exposed. Let me start with environ.py. I need to see helper functions _int etc. Let me read lines 1-30 of environ.py to check helpers... The read_range results earlier were delivered (1-30). But content isn't shown in current excerpt. Let me read the first 30 lines again to be safe? Reading is fine (not a full re-read requirement issue). Actually evidence showed lines 1-30 read twice already. I don't have the content though. Let me just do a targeted read of lines 1-30 and logger.py 40-120 maybe. Keep it minimal: read environ.py lines 1-30 to see helpers.\n\nThen edits:\n\nenviron.py: add descriptor factory method? Task says descriptor class may add helper factory methods, config item must follow existing mechanism: first access reads env, then cached; unset -> None; set -> int parse, >=32, ValueError containing variable name.\n\nThe error wrapping in EnvVar.__get__ already prepends name. But TypeError conversion... For our case, if empty string: int(\"\") raises ValueError -> wrapped as \"NAME: ...\" good.\n\nAdd to EnvVar:\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def positive_int_or_none(*, minimum: int = 32) -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        def _init(v: str | None) -> int | None:\n            if v is None:\n                return None\n            value = int(v)\n            if value < minimum:\n                raise ValueError(f\"{value} < {minimum}\")\n            return value\n        return EnvVar(_init)\n\nAnd in envs under -- Logging --:\n    DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar.positive_int_or_none(minimum=32)\n\nWait but empty string \"\" — int(\"\") raises ValueError, wrapped fine.\n\nlogger.py: extend NewLineFormatter __init__ with max_message_chars keyword-only. Validate: if not None: must be int and not bool and >=32 else ValueError. Add _OMIT_MARKER = \"...<omitted>...\". Truncation applied after parameter substitution — record.message is set by super().format(record) (using record.getMessage()). In format(), super().format(record) sets record.message = record.getMessage() then formats. We must not mutate record.msg etc. We can compute truncated message separately and replace in formatted msg? Requirement: budget only limits message body, not prefix/time. Approach: after super().format(record) -> msg includes prefix and message (and exception traceback appended after message). Simpler approach: compute message = record.getMessage(); if len > budget, build truncated; then format using a copy of record? But must keep exception/stack_info handling and multi-line prefix logic which uses record.message.\n\nCleanest: temporarily... no, cannot mutate shared record. Use logging.makeLogRecord? Deepcopy of record? That would also copy exc_info tuple references (fine, doesn't mutate). But copying record: `copy.copy`? logging.Formatter.format also sets record.message. Hmm.\n\nAlternative: make a shallow copy of the record with copy.copy, set copy.message = truncated, then call super().format(copy). But super().format will call record.getMessage() again and overwrite copy.message with full message (msg + args). That breaks. Unless we set copy.msg = truncated, copy.args = (). That mutates only the copy, not shared record. That's allowed (\"不能改写共享原记录的 msg\"). So:\n\nimport copy\nrecord_copy = copy.copy(record)\nrecord_copy.msg = truncated_message\nrecord_copy.args = ()\nthen msg = super().format(record_copy). But then formatTime uses record_copy.created — same. Exception info: record_copy.exc_info shared tuple — formatException doesn't mutate. stack_info same. Good.\n\nBut careful: the multi-line alignment check uses record.message — after super().format, record_copy.message is set = getMessage() = truncated, so use record_copy.message.\n\nAlso need record.keep_full_message exactly True: `if getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is True: skip truncation`.\n\nNote: If exc_text present etc. Fine.\n\nAlso appended exception text is part of msg but not truncated (since we only truncate the message body). Good.\n\nImplementation in format:\n\n    def format(self, record):\n        if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            message = record.getMessage()\n            if len(message) > self.max_message_chars:\n                budget = self.max_message_chars - len(_OMIT_MARKER)\n                head = (budget + 1) // 2\n                tail = budget - head\n                truncated = f\"{message[:head]}{_OMIT_MARKER}{message[len(message)-tail:] if tail else ''}\"\n                record = copy.copy(record)\n                record.msg = truncated\n                record.args = ()\n        msg = super().format(record)\n        ...\n\nWait budget when max=32: 32-15=17? Marker \"...<omitted>...\" length: 3 dots + 9 chars \"<omitted>\" = 9? \"<omitted>\" is 9 characters (< o m i t t e d > = 9). plus \"...\" \"...\" = 6 → 15. So budget 17, head = ceil(17/2)=9, tail=8. Matches example. Good.\n\nBeware tail slicing when tail==0: message[-0:] returns whole message — guard. With budget>=17, head>=9,tail>=8, fine but guard anyway.\n\nAlso `self.max_message_chars` must be set before super().__init__? no.\n\nValidation:\n\n        if max_message_chars is not None:\n            if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int):\n                raise ValueError(f\"max_message_chars must be an int >= 32 or None, got {max_message_chars!r}\")\n            if max_message_chars < 32:\n                raise ValueError(...)\n\nBut default init reading config: _setup_logger creates NewLineFormatter(..., max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS). Careful: reading the config each init. If process env has invalid value, ValueError on init — acceptable.\n\nHmm, but reading config in _setup_logger at import time: logger module import triggers _setup_logger at module bottom presumably. Invalid env var would break import — tests likely check? The test says errors raised from config access. Risk: tests might set invalid env then import logger... probably test config only. Keep it.\n\nBut careful: existing tests may import logger with env unset → None → fine.\n\nstats_logger.py: `logger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True})`.\n\nWait — logger.info with `%` in message? extra fine.\n\nNow let's check exact line in stats_logger and whether logger.info receives only m.\n\nLet me read the relevant snippets to confirm and get exact strings. Evidence shows:\n\n```python\n            for m in msgs:\n                logger.info(m)\n```\n\nNow also check `_setup_logger` and imports. Let me do a quick read of logger.py 60-110 to confirm exact text.\n\nActually evidence gives enough: _setup_logger body and NewLineFormatter. I'll read environ.py 1-30 for helpers to place the factory method cleanly.\n\nLet's act."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":10066,"completion_tokens":1629,"total_tokens":11695,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":4096},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1572},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":4096,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":5970},"system_fingerprint":"bc19ba97cbe016479903222bb1578db6"}